  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Daldinia childiae is a wood-decomposing fungus in the Hypoxylaceae family, characterized by its dark, crusty fruiting bodies that develop on dead hardwood. This species plays an important ecological role as a saprotroph, breaking down woody debris in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Dark Crusty Stromata",
      "description": "Forms hard, black or dark brown crusty structures on wood surface, often with a wrinkled or lumpy appearance",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Preference",
      "description": "Typically found on dead branches and logs of hardwood trees, particularly oak and birch",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Release",
      "description": "Produces numerous small, dark perithecia embedded in the stroma that release spores through tiny openings",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Daldinia childiae colonizes dead and decaying hardwood in moist forest environments throughout New England, playing a crucial role in nutrient cycling.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus can remain viable on dead wood for extended periods, slowly decomposing the cellulose and lignin, and its fruiting bodies are so hard they were historically used as natural tinder for starting fires.",
